# This repository stores LF, and every working tree should too.
#
# Until this file existed the convention was held only by habit. Git for Windows sets
# core.autocrlf=true in its SYSTEM config (C:/Program Files/Git/etc/gitconfig), so a fresh
# clone checks text files out as CRLF and git announces the intent on every command:
# "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it". The stored blobs stayed LF
# regardless, because nothing had yet written one back through that conversion -- which is
# luck, not a policy, and the kind of luck that ends with one Windows checkout committing a
# whole file as CRLF and burying a one-line change in a 200-line diff.
#
# eol=lf settles it for everyone, and beats asking each developer to set core.autocrlf,
# because it travels with the repository. Adding it changed nothing: `git add --renormalize .`
# reported no modified file, which is the check worth repeating if this line is ever edited.
#
# No CRLF exceptions. Nothing tracked here needs them -- the one .ps1 reads LF fine, and there
# are no .bat/.cmd, which are the formats with a real claim to CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary: never converted, never diffed as text. `text=auto` above already detects these, so
# this is belt-and-braces for the types actually tracked, where a stray conversion would
# corrupt the file rather than merely churn the diff.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.ttf binary
